Reintroduces the bug fixed in "UI/AppKit: Fix pasting by comparing pasteboard types by value".

NSPasteboardType is an NSString. Comparing with == tests pointer identity, which only holds when
this same process wrote the pasteboard. Types written by another application are reconstructed as
distinct objects, so every such paste produced no representations at all.

Used by Meta/check-test-potency.sh to prove Tests/UI/AppKit/TestClipboard.mm detects this.

diff --git a/UI/AppKit/Application/Clipboard.mm b/UI/AppKit/Application/Clipboard.mm
--- a/UI/AppKit/Application/Clipboard.mm
+++ b/UI/AppKit/Application/Clipboard.mm
@@ -19,11 +19,11 @@ namespace Ladybird {
 {
     // NB: The types reported by the pasteboard are deserialized from the pasteboard server, so they are not the same
     //     objects as the NSPasteboardType constants. They must be compared by value, not by identity.
-    if ([type isEqualToString:NSPasteboardTypeString])
+    if (type == NSPasteboardTypeString)
         return "text/plain"_string;
-    if ([type isEqualToString:NSPasteboardTypeHTML])
+    if (type == NSPasteboardTypeHTML)
         return "text/html"_string;
-    if ([type isEqualToString:NSPasteboardTypePNG])
+    if (type == NSPasteboardTypePNG)
         return "image/png"_string;
     return {};
 }
